Based on the provided full-text excerpt, the authors run broad evaluations across model families, datasets, and benchmarks, compare existing diversity-oriented SFT objectives in a unified setup, and separately test focal loss as an SFT objective. The qualitative conclusion is that TOFU improves generative diversity over standard CE while keeping output quality competitive. Focal loss alone already helps with low-frequency pattern coverage, but without the tempered reference term it is not sufficient to reliably preserve pretrained knowledge.

However, the excerpt does not include the full experimental tables, model sizes, training-token counts, benchmark names, or exact numerical gains, so it would be unsafe to invent precise improvements. The robust takeaways are:
1. diversity collapse under standard SFT appears consistently across multiple settings rather than as a model-specific artifact;
2. quality-only evaluation can hide distributional narrowing, so diversity and quality must be measured jointly;
3. combining rare-pattern reweighting with reference preservation works better than either ingredient alone.

To extract exact numbers, one needs the main result tables, ablations, and the precise definition of the diversity metrics.
